<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sapienza University of Rome</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1303">1303</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as La Sapienza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sapienza University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> is a public university in Rome, founded in 1303 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> as a studium for the papal city. One of the largest universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, it is known for archaeology and Near Eastern studies; its mission under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-matthiae">Paolo Matthiae</a> began excavating Tell Mardikh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1964 and identified the site as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> in 1968.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sapienza-university-of-rome.jpg" alt="Sapienza University of Rome" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Melirius · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sapienza_entrance_(20040201351).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The university was founded by the papal bull In Supremae praeminentia Dignitatis, issued on 20 April 1303 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a>, as a Studium for ecclesiastical studies more under his control than the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. This made it the first pontifical university. In 1431 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a> reorganised the studium with the bull In supremae, granting masters and students the broadest privileges and decreeing that it should include the four schools of Law, Medicine, Philosophy and Theology. He introduced a new tax on wine to raise funds, and the money bought a palace that later housed the church of Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1527 ended the university's days of splendour: the studium was closed, some professors were killed and others dispersed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> restored it shortly after his election in 1534. In the 1650s it became known as Sapienza, meaning wisdom or knowledge, a name it retains.</p><p>The Alessandrina University Library was built in 1667 by Pope Alexander VII. In 1703 Pope Clement XI bought land on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janiculum">Janiculum</a> with his private funds and created a botanical garden, which became the best known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through the labours of the Trionfetti brothers. Filippo Maria Renazzi wrote the first complete history of the university in 1803-1806.</p><p>In 1870 Sapienza stopped being the papal university and became the university of the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and it expanded rapidly afterwards. The new campus, planned by Marcello Piacentini, was completed in 1935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The main campus is the Città Universitaria, covering 44 hectares near the Tiburtina Station, and the university has 50 libraries, 19 museums and a botanical garden. Its alumni and teachers include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-montessori">Maria Montessori</a>, the future pope Eugenio Pacelli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luigi-pirandello">Luigi Pirandello</a> and Nobel laureates such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-marconi">Guglielmo Marconi</a>, who taught there in 1935-1937, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enrico-fermi">Enrico Fermi</a>, who held the chair of theoretical physics from 1926 to 1938. Among its scholars are the mathematician Vito Volterra, the orientalist Giuseppe Tucci and the archaeologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-matthiae">Paolo Matthiae</a>, director of the excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a>. It is the largest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sapienza University of Rome” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An Italian mission from the <b>University of Rome</b> led by Paolo Matthiae began excavating in 1964, and in 1968 a statue dedicated by a king named Ibbit-Lim, whose inscription mentioned Ebla, identified the site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
